# Gemma Dispatcher — the ONE file to edit for a model swap, retune, or
# a llama.cpp upgrade. Apply changes with: gemma-dispatcher-apply
#
# Values below were established by benchmarking on this machine (2026-08-23).
# See the notes against each before changing them.
#
# 2026-08-23, later: swapped 12B QAT -> E4B QAT after a head-to-head on the
# dispatcher's own 17 tools (19 tasks x 2 reps, real system prompt, real
# tool loop). With the tool schemas corrected the two scored 100% (12B) vs
# 97% (E4B) -- one task in 38 -- while E4B ran 2.7x faster and 3.8 GB lighter:
#   12B QAT : 100%  7.96 tok/s  4.3 s/task  8.5 GB RSS
#   E4B QAT :  97% 21.86 tok/s  1.5 s/task  4.7 GB RSS
# Most of the pre-fix gap was the SCHEMAS in tools.py, not the model: missing
# enums let both models invent service names, and the gpu_model_set_config
# example named a card ("Vulkan2") this machine does not have.

# llama.cpp build. Must be new enough to know the 'gemma4-assistant'
# architecture (MTP drafter) — b10588 or later. Older builds fail with
# "unknown model architecture: 'gemma4-assistant'".
GEMMA_LLAMA_BIN=$HOME/.local/opt/llama-b10588/llama-b10588/llama-server
GEMMA_LLAMA_LIB_DIR=$HOME/.local/opt/llama-b10588/llama-b10588

# Weights + the multi-token-prediction drafter that sits beside them.
GEMMA_MODEL_PATH=$HOME/models/unsloth/gemma-4-E4B-it-qat-GGUF/gemma-4-E4B-it-qat-UD-Q4_K_XL.gguf
GEMMA_DRAFT_PATH=$HOME/models/unsloth/gemma-4-E4B-it-qat-GGUF/mtp-gemma-4-E4B-it.gguf

# Vision projector. Comment out for text-only. Costs ~1 GB of RAM on top of
# the weights. NOTE: the chat page at :8097 has no image-upload control, so
# this only reaches anything that POSTs an image_url to /v1/chat/completions.
GEMMA_MMPROJ=$HOME/models/unsloth/gemma-4-E4B-it-qat-GGUF/mmproj-F16.gguf

# Google's canonical template (published 2026-07-09). Fixes tool-call loops
# and turn closures — worth up to 10% tool reliability over a stale one.
# Each model gets its OWN canonical template; this is E4B's.
GEMMA_CHAT_TEMPLATE=$HOME/models/gemma-4-E4B-it/chat_template.jinja

# Context. RE-MEASURED 2026-08-23; the old "377 MB per 1K" figure was wrong.
# Gemma 4 runs 40 of its 48 layers on 1024-token sliding-window attention, so
# only 8 full-attention layers scale with context length. Measured on this box:
#   load footprint  ctx 4096 -> 7914 MB | 16384 -> 7982 MB | 32768 -> 8125 MB
#   8002 real tokens of KV cost 513 MB  => ~64 MB per 1K, not 377 MB
# So peak at 12288 is ~8.7 GB, comfortably inside the 12 GB ceiling.
#
# The per-1K figures above were measured on the 12B. E4B has fewer layers and
# a smaller KV, and loads at ~4.7 GB RSS at ctx 12288, so 12288 is comfortable
# with room to spare. Not re-measured per-1K for E4B.
#
# Context is NOT what threatens this machine — the weights are.
# See LimitMEMLOCK / --mlock: the real failure mode is Gemma being swapped
# out when a big model loads on the GPUs, not the KV cache overflowing.
GEMMA_CTX=12288

# KV cache precision. Was q8_0; moved to f16 on 2026-08-23.
# Gemma 4 is a documented exception to "q8_0 KV is practically lossless":
# published KL divergence vs an f16-KV reference is 0.108 (31B dense) and
# 0.377 (26B A4B) where Qwen 3.6 stays under 0.04. E4B was not in that test,
# so its sensitivity is simply unknown.
# And the saving is not worth finding out: E4B's GGUF says only 7 of its 42
# layers use full attention (sliding_window_pattern = 1 in 6, window 512), so
# at ctx 12288 the whole KV is ~371 MB at f16 vs ~197 MB at q8_0 -- about
# 175 MB. Not worth any quality risk on 16 GB with 4+ GB free.
GEMMA_CACHE_TYPE_K=f16
GEMMA_CACHE_TYPE_V=f16

# 6 = physical cores. 12 (hyperthreads) measured ~15% SLOWER on decode.
GEMMA_THREADS=6

# Draft depth. Measured on this CPU FOR THE 12B: n=2 -> 5.57, n=3 -> 9.99,
# n=4 -> 7.49, so 3 was the peak. NOT re-swept for E4B — E4B hits 21.9 tok/s
# at n=3, but the optimum may sit elsewhere. Worth a sweep if you care.
GEMMA_SPEC_N_MAX=3

GEMMA_PORT=8098

# Thinking. Gemma 4 reasons at length before answering, which is great for
# hard questions and wasteful for "launch this app". Measured on this box:
#   thinking on  -> hundreds of reasoning tokens, ~30-60s for a tool call
#   thinking off -> 15 tokens, 3.7s, same correct tool call
# "false" is the right default for a dispatcher. Set "true" if you'd rather
# it reason through harder requests and accept the wait.
GEMMA_THINKING=false
